{
  /*
    Type-check the backend: npx tsc -p tsconfig.server.json --noEmit

    ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Nothing was checking `server/`. It runs under `tsx`, which transpiles and does not type-check, and
    the whole-repo config next door covers each app's sources and the libraries only — so a type error in a route
    was found at runtime, by whoever called it, if they called it.

    That went unnoticed for as long as the backend was four small files over local JSON. It stops being
    acceptable at the point the backend opens connections to somebody's production database.

    ── AND WHY IT IS NOT JUST ADDED TO tsconfig.app.json ───────────────────────────────────
    That config sets `"types": []`, which is right for browser code — an app that can see Node's globals
    can accidentally use them, and `process.env` in a component is a build that works and a page that
    breaks. Server code needs exactly those globals. Two configs, two type environments, one command in
    `npm run typecheck` that runs both.
  */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "./out-tsc/server",
    "types": ["node"],
    // The backend is ESM (`import.meta.url` in config.ts) and tsx transpiles it to CJS at run time.
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["server/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["**/*.spec.ts"],
  /*
    Both overridden explicitly, because `extends` inherits them.

    The root config is a solution file: `"files": []` and two `references`. Inheriting those made this
    project compile the Angular apps through the reference graph, with this file's server-flavoured
    compiler options — which reports every Material import as missing and is nothing to do with the
    backend. It is also why `tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` silently checks NOTHING and always passes.
  */
  "files": [],
  "references": []
}
